window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2025-06-1", "source": "2025年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university is seeking students' opinions on the necessity of making College Chinese a compulsory course. You are now to write an essay to express your view. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "The question of whether College Chinese should be a compulsory course has sparked heated discussion on\ncampus. In my view, making it compulsory is both necessary and wise.\nAdmittedly, some students argue that Chinese is their mother tongue and needs no further study. Yet this view\noverlooks two facts. First, a solid command of Chinese sharpens our thinking and expression. Many science\nmajors write reports full of grammatical errors and muddled logic, which weakens their careers. A required\ncourse would help them communicate clearly. What's more, classical Chinese carries our cultural heritage.\nThrough reading poems and essays, students inherit values and a sense of identity that no other subject offers.\nTherefore, I firmly believe College Chinese deserves a place as a compulsory course. It is not a burden but an\ninvestment in both our language ability and our cultural roots."}, "translation": {"prompt": "被誉为\"杂交水稻(hybrid rice)之父\"的袁隆平和他的科研团队克服重重困难，研发出了一种超级杂交水稻。这项技术获得了举世公认的巨大成功。通过这项技术的应用，水稻抗旱抗病能力更强，能适应不同的气候和土壤条件，产量可提高20-30%。超级杂交水稻营养丰富，口感更佳。目前，这项技术已经在许多国家得到广泛应用，为全球粮食安全做出了重大贡献。", "sample": "Yuan Longping, known as the father of hybrid rice, and his research team, overcoming numerous difficulties, developed a super hybrid rice. This technology has achieved great success recognized worldwide. With the application of this technology, the rice has stronger resistance to drought and disease and can adapt to different climates and soil conditions, and its yield can increase by 20 to 30 percent. In addition, super hybrid rice is rich in nutrition and tastes better. Currently, this technology has been widely applied in many countries, making great contributions to global food security."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2025-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Psychologists have long been interested in how we construct our identities and the sorts of strategies that we use to present ourselves in society. New findings suggest that the kinds of  __26__  and strategic self-presentation behaviour we see in adults appear at a much younger age than  __27__  known.\nResearch shows that a child's awareness of social standing comes from adults. Like grown-ups, kids want to be  __28__  by those they admire. Interactive experiences may provide opportunities for children to learn about what  __29__  a desirable reputation and the kinds of strategies that are effective for  __30__  a good reputation in their social environment.\nFive-year-olds aren't just aware of their reputations, they also behave strategically to alter their outward image. They will  __31__  their behaviour in order to appear moral or socially good in the eyes of key observers. While we know that adults use a large variety of attributes to manage and create impressions, we don't yet know whether children  __32__  and use the fact that different attributes are valuable at different times to different  __33__  . It's important for us to further consider where in this process children succeed in controlling their reputation and where they  __34__  .\nA question to think about is, \"What happens even earlier than age five?\" Children don't just show up to the first day of kindergarten and have the idea of reputation pop  __35__  into existence. The logical question to ask is, \"What happens even earlier?\"", "matchIntro": "Getting young minds into design", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Recently, a leading design federation in the UK warned that the UK could face a creative skills shortage, after the latest figures revealed a drop of almost 10 percent in students studying Design and Technology. These figures showed that the number of pupils that sat exams in Design and Technology fell 10 percent from 204,788 to 185,279 last year."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Chief executive John Kampfner of the Creative Industries Federation thinks that this drop in figures has worrying implications for the skills pipeline in Britain's hugely successful arts and creative industries. He said, \"We already have skills shortages in many jobs such as animation(动画制作) and special effects.\" He added: \"Engineering, which requires a similar mix of creative and technical skills, also has recruitment problems.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "Many other designers and architects have expressed concern over the future supply of home-grown talent for UK firms, and industry bodies have pointed out that there has been a lack of roots-level support. However, if these designers had visited the Design Museum shop in London earlier this year their fears may have been eased. With £10 to spend, they could have bought a fascinating children's toy called \"Dazzle Racer\". An automotive cylinder(圆柱体), it included a wind-up, elastic(有弹性的)-band-powered mechanism, minimal parts, all 100 percent recycled, and lots of stickers. It was good fun, simple, eye-catching and very original."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Well, you'd expect the Design Museum to commission and make some interesting items, but this one was different. Designed by a group of six Year 9 and Year 10 boys from Finchley Catholic High School, the toy was the winning entry in the museum's 2015 Design Ventura competition, which brings the business of design to life for students aged 13 to 16 by challenging them to develop a new creative, sustainable and commercially feasible product for the Design Museum shop and attracts hundreds of entries nationwide."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"We did Design Ventura in my previous school,\" says Liam Hourican, Finchley Catholic High School's design technology curriculum leader. \"Then when I changed schools four years ago, I introduced it here because it helps to develop so many skills.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "The theme for last year's competition was \"Move\", and Hourican began with three groups working in lunch breaks and after school before selecting the group with the most innovative idea. Each participating school may submit just one proposal. \"It's the taking part and doing the work which is important,\" he says. \"And I never dreamt we'd win.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "Catherine Ritman Smith, head of learning at the Design Museum, is expecting around 10,000 participants aged 13 to 16 to take part this year and says that the project—funded by Deutsche Bank as part of its youth engagement programme Born To Be—is the biggest event in the museum's calendar. \"We started Design Ventura with around 800 young people in 2010 and it has grown steadily since,\" she says. \"Teachers like it because the skills are all transferable and it helps to validate the value of design as a subject,\" she explains, telling me that nearly 600 schools have taken part so far."}, {"label": "H", "text": "So how does the competition work? A single-word theme is announced in the summer. This year it is \"Change\". Schools work with groups to produce imaginative design ideas for a product in simple materials that could sell for £10 in the Design Museum shop. Participating schools then register by November and eventually submit their design idea. Along the way there is training and support for teachers and tutoring for students from designers. An additional bonus is when the winners see their design on sale in the spring of the following year."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Hourican and his pupils have happy memories of spending a whole day at the museum, having been selected as one of 10 shortlisted schools to present their idea to a panel of judges, including designer Jasper Conran. Then they worked with the Kin Design Studio in Shoreditch, met designers at their school and attended a lunch with Deutsche Bank employees. The students helped to make decisions and there were plenty of discussions—they changed the product's name, for example."}, {"label": "J", "text": "\"One of the really useful things about this competition,\" says Hourican, \"is that there's a commercial element because the product is going on sale for real and the boys had to learn about budgeting and marketing, as well as designing their game.\" Profits go to charity. \"The winning team chooses where it wants the profits to go,\" says Ritman Smith. \"The Finchley Catholic High School product raised about £1,000 for Great Ormond Street Hospital.\" Winning entries in past years have included a make-your-own cloth kit called Dove Bunting and a threefold, wallet-sized travel game with goals at each end, called Badoiiing."}, {"label": "K", "text": "It costs the schools nothing to participate in Design Ventura; the professional designers provide their services for free. Among them are architect Asif Khan, a Design Museum trustee who has worked as designer-in-residence at the museum, helping emerging architects. He has recently been commissioned to design the new Museum of London building in Smithfield."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Another is television presenter and interior designer Naomi Cleaver, who is one of the competition's judges. \"I'm very keen to encourage young people,\" she says, observing that design brings together other subjects such as literature, art, history, geography and science. \"Now that design education is limited in the curriculum, I'm all in favour of competitions such as Design Ventura, which helps to bring the standard curriculum to life,\" says Cleaver. \"And the judging day is terrific fun. Some of these students are very imaginative. One group presented their idea in the form of a puppet show and we see some marvelous demonstration models. I'm always impressed by the levels of confidence and the support the students get from their teachers.\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "Ritman Smith adds: \"Design technology has become a tricky subject to make a case for, and we've heard of departments closing in some schools. We find that if pupils take part in Design Ventura in Year Nine it can be the trigger which leads them to opt for it at General Certificate of Secondary Education, so we are helping to keep alive something which is crucial to industry and entrepreneurship.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "New research suggests that pandas may be at risk of dying out because they are too comfortable. Experts say too much happiness can stop the bears from searching for new mates.\nEnvironmentalists have long believed that building roads or homes near the bears may threaten their survival by \"reducing or fragmenting their natural: habitats\", The Times reported. But the new research suggests that a \"modest degree of discomfort and fragmentation\" may actually help preserve panda populations.\nThe research was conducted by scientists from Michigan State University. It concluded that pandas fail to wander off in search of new mates if they find their habitat too comfortable, resulting in a lack of vital genetic diversity.\nFor their study—outlined in a paper in the journal Conservation Biology—the team looked at genetic diversity and spread among a Chinese panda population. The ideal level of perfectly livable habitat was found to be only 80% of an area, with the remainder either too harsh or too affected by human activity.\nThe experts concluded that pandas should ideally \"be happy enough to thrive, but not so content that they don't want to move around and find new mates\".\nTheir conclusions about what The Guardian described as this \"sweet spot\" are in line with the so-called Goldilocks principle: that there can be just the right amount of something. The concept has been applied to a wide range of disciplines, from developmental psychology to economics and engineering.\nClaudio Sillero, a professor of conservation biology at Oxford University, told the newspaper that the new findings could have implications beyond panda conservation.\n\"Most large animals that eat meat live in increasingly fragmented landscapes,\" said Sillero, who was not involved in the research. \"It may well be that the messy nature of their relationship with human efforts induces more animals to scatter or travel further, and might result in greater genetic connectivity and enhanced population persistence.\"\nThe most recent count of pandas found that there were more than 1,800 left in the wild, putting them on the list of vulnerable, but not endangered, species.", "47": "New research suggests that pandas may be at risk of dying out because they are too comfortable. Experts say too much happiness can stop the bears from searching for new mates.\nEnvironmentalists have long believed that building roads or homes near the bears may threaten their survival by \"reducing or fragmenting their natural: habitats\", The Times reported. But the new research suggests that a \"modest degree of discomfort and fragmentation\" may actually help preserve panda populations.\nThe research was conducted by scientists from Michigan State University. It concluded that pandas fail to wander off in search of new mates if they find their habitat too comfortable, resulting in a lack of vital genetic diversity.\nFor their study—outlined in a paper in the journal Conservation Biology—the team looked at genetic diversity and spread among a Chinese panda population. The ideal level of perfectly livable habitat was found to be only 80% of an area, with the remainder either too harsh or too affected by human activity.\nThe experts concluded that pandas should ideally \"be happy enough to thrive, but not so content that they don't want to move around and find new mates\".\nTheir conclusions about what The Guardian described as this \"sweet spot\" are in line with the so-called Goldilocks principle: that there can be just the right amount of something. The concept has been applied to a wide range of disciplines, from developmental psychology to economics and engineering.\nClaudio Sillero, a professor of conservation biology at Oxford University, told the newspaper that the new findings could have implications beyond panda conservation.\n\"Most large animals that eat meat live in increasingly fragmented landscapes,\" said Sillero, who was not involved in the research. \"It may well be that the messy nature of their relationship with human efforts induces more animals to scatter or travel further, and might result in greater genetic connectivity and enhanced population persistence.\"\nThe most recent count of pandas found that there were more than 1,800 left in the wild, putting them on the list of vulnerable, but not endangered, species.", "48": "New research suggests that pandas may be at risk of dying out because they are too comfortable. Experts say too much happiness can stop the bears from searching for new mates.\nEnvironmentalists have long believed that building roads or homes near the bears may threaten their survival by \"reducing or fragmenting their natural: habitats\", The Times reported. But the new research suggests that a \"modest degree of discomfort and fragmentation\" may actually help preserve panda populations.\nThe research was conducted by scientists from Michigan State University. It concluded that pandas fail to wander off in search of new mates if they find their habitat too comfortable, resulting in a lack of vital genetic diversity.\nFor their study—outlined in a paper in the journal Conservation Biology—the team looked at genetic diversity and spread among a Chinese panda population. The ideal level of perfectly livable habitat was found to be only 80% of an area, with the remainder either too harsh or too affected by human activity.\nThe experts concluded that pandas should ideally \"be happy enough to thrive, but not so content that they don't want to move around and find new mates\".\nTheir conclusions about what The Guardian described as this \"sweet spot\" are in line with the so-called Goldilocks principle: that there can be just the right amount of something. The concept has been applied to a wide range of disciplines, from developmental psychology to economics and engineering.\nClaudio Sillero, a professor of conservation biology at Oxford University, told the newspaper that the new findings could have implications beyond panda conservation.\n\"Most large animals that eat meat live in increasingly fragmented landscapes,\" said Sillero, who was not involved in the research. \"It may well be that the messy nature of their relationship with human efforts induces more animals to scatter or travel further, and might result in greater genetic connectivity and enhanced population persistence.\"\nThe most recent count of pandas found that there were more than 1,800 left in the wild, putting them on the list of vulnerable, but not endangered, species.", "49": "New research suggests that pandas may be at risk of dying out because they are too comfortable. Experts say too much happiness can stop the bears from searching for new mates.\nEnvironmentalists have long believed that building roads or homes near the bears may threaten their survival by \"reducing or fragmenting their natural: habitats\", The Times reported. But the new research suggests that a \"modest degree of discomfort and fragmentation\" may actually help preserve panda populations.\nThe research was conducted by scientists from Michigan State University. It concluded that pandas fail to wander off in search of new mates if they find their habitat too comfortable, resulting in a lack of vital genetic diversity.\nFor their study—outlined in a paper in the journal Conservation Biology—the team looked at genetic diversity and spread among a Chinese panda population. The ideal level of perfectly livable habitat was found to be only 80% of an area, with the remainder either too harsh or too affected by human activity.\nThe experts concluded that pandas should ideally \"be happy enough to thrive, but not so content that they don't want to move around and find new mates\".\nTheir conclusions about what The Guardian described as this \"sweet spot\" are in line with the so-called Goldilocks principle: that there can be just the right amount of something. The concept has been applied to a wide range of disciplines, from developmental psychology to economics and engineering.\nClaudio Sillero, a professor of conservation biology at Oxford University, told the newspaper that the new findings could have implications beyond panda conservation.\n\"Most large animals that eat meat live in increasingly fragmented landscapes,\" said Sillero, who was not involved in the research. \"It may well be that the messy nature of their relationship with human efforts induces more animals to scatter or travel further, and might result in greater genetic connectivity and enhanced population persistence.\"\nThe most recent count of pandas found that there were more than 1,800 left in the wild, putting them on the list of vulnerable, but not endangered, species.", "50": "New research suggests that pandas may be at risk of dying out because they are too comfortable. Experts say too much happiness can stop the bears from searching for new mates.\nEnvironmentalists have long believed that building roads or homes near the bears may threaten their survival by \"reducing or fragmenting their natural: habitats\", The Times reported. But the new research suggests that a \"modest degree of discomfort and fragmentation\" may actually help preserve panda populations.\nThe research was conducted by scientists from Michigan State University. It concluded that pandas fail to wander off in search of new mates if they find their habitat too comfortable, resulting in a lack of vital genetic diversity.\nFor their study—outlined in a paper in the journal Conservation Biology—the team looked at genetic diversity and spread among a Chinese panda population. The ideal level of perfectly livable habitat was found to be only 80% of an area, with the remainder either too harsh or too affected by human activity.\nThe experts concluded that pandas should ideally \"be happy enough to thrive, but not so content that they don't want to move around and find new mates\".\nTheir conclusions about what The Guardian described as this \"sweet spot\" are in line with the so-called Goldilocks principle: that there can be just the right amount of something. The concept has been applied to a wide range of disciplines, from developmental psychology to economics and engineering.\nClaudio Sillero, a professor of conservation biology at Oxford University, told the newspaper that the new findings could have implications beyond panda conservation.\n\"Most large animals that eat meat live in increasingly fragmented landscapes,\" said Sillero, who was not involved in the research. \"It may well be that the messy nature of their relationship with human efforts induces more animals to scatter or travel further, and might result in greater genetic connectivity and enhanced population persistence.\"\nThe most recent count of pandas found that there were more than 1,800 left in the wild, putting them on the list of vulnerable, but not endangered, species.", "51": "With those born with natural talents, it feels as if they excel without really trying. But what about those of us who don't have a natural talent? We've been told all our lives that if you work hard, you too can succeed. But with the release of Angela Duckworth's Grit, we are given a new key to success.\n\"As much as talent counts, effort counts twice,\" says Duckworth in Grit. She introduces a new concept that talent may be overrated, and if you want real success, what you need is grit, the perfect combination of passion and persistence. Even if you have natural talent, it's nothing without grit.\nDuckworth says grit is the difference between success and failure. A person who has grit is more likely to succeed than a person who does not. When we think about attaining success—whether it's landing that job or learning that new skill—our thoughts are immediately burdened by all the things we must first learn. If you want that new job, you have to learn the job skills, then the interview skills, then the dress part—and you must be perfect at all of them. Grit is different because it tells us that perfection isn't the goal.\nGrit lifts the unreasonable expectations off our shoulders. Grit tells us that the door is open wider than we first thought possible. Grit allows us to redefine our goals. Think about it: what's something you've always wanted to do, but gave up because you \"don't have the skills for it\"? What's something you love but aren't good at?\nThe real workings of grit are to have sustainable passion and continue to try. Effort means more than your natural ability. Even if you haven't mastered a skill, grit tells you that you can still succeed if you can transform your passion into action. In a way, Duckworth is giving new hope to people who have shut the doors on their dreams. She is saying it is possible that you can accomplish anything. If at first you fail, then try one more time with grit.", "52": "With those born with natural talents, it feels as if they excel without really trying. But what about those of us who don't have a natural talent? We've been told all our lives that if you work hard, you too can succeed. But with the release of Angela Duckworth's Grit, we are given a new key to success.\n\"As much as talent counts, effort counts twice,\" says Duckworth in Grit. She introduces a new concept that talent may be overrated, and if you want real success, what you need is grit, the perfect combination of passion and persistence. Even if you have natural talent, it's nothing without grit.\nDuckworth says grit is the difference between success and failure. A person who has grit is more likely to succeed than a person who does not. When we think about attaining success—whether it's landing that job or learning that new skill—our thoughts are immediately burdened by all the things we must first learn. If you want that new job, you have to learn the job skills, then the interview skills, then the dress part—and you must be perfect at all of them. Grit is different because it tells us that perfection isn't the goal.\nGrit lifts the unreasonable expectations off our shoulders. Grit tells us that the door is open wider than we first thought possible. Grit allows us to redefine our goals. Think about it: what's something you've always wanted to do, but gave up because you \"don't have the skills for it\"? What's something you love but aren't good at?\nThe real workings of grit are to have sustainable passion and continue to try. Effort means more than your natural ability. Even if you haven't mastered a skill, grit tells you that you can still succeed if you can transform your passion into action. In a way, Duckworth is giving new hope to people who have shut the doors on their dreams. She is saying it is possible that you can accomplish anything. If at first you fail, then try one more time with grit.", "53": "With those born with natural talents, it feels as if they excel without really trying. But what about those of us who don't have a natural talent? We've been told all our lives that if you work hard, you too can succeed. But with the release of Angela Duckworth's Grit, we are given a new key to success.\n\"As much as talent counts, effort counts twice,\" says Duckworth in Grit. She introduces a new concept that talent may be overrated, and if you want real success, what you need is grit, the perfect combination of passion and persistence. Even if you have natural talent, it's nothing without grit.\nDuckworth says grit is the difference between success and failure. A person who has grit is more likely to succeed than a person who does not. When we think about attaining success—whether it's landing that job or learning that new skill—our thoughts are immediately burdened by all the things we must first learn. If you want that new job, you have to learn the job skills, then the interview skills, then the dress part—and you must be perfect at all of them. Grit is different because it tells us that perfection isn't the goal.\nGrit lifts the unreasonable expectations off our shoulders. Grit tells us that the door is open wider than we first thought possible. Grit allows us to redefine our goals. Think about it: what's something you've always wanted to do, but gave up because you \"don't have the skills for it\"? What's something you love but aren't good at?\nThe real workings of grit are to have sustainable passion and continue to try. Effort means more than your natural ability. Even if you haven't mastered a skill, grit tells you that you can still succeed if you can transform your passion into action. In a way, Duckworth is giving new hope to people who have shut the doors on their dreams. She is saying it is possible that you can accomplish anything. If at first you fail, then try one more time with grit.", "54": "With those born with natural talents, it feels as if they excel without really trying. But what about those of us who don't have a natural talent? We've been told all our lives that if you work hard, you too can succeed. But with the release of Angela Duckworth's Grit, we are given a new key to success.\n\"As much as talent counts, effort counts twice,\" says Duckworth in Grit. She introduces a new concept that talent may be overrated, and if you want real success, what you need is grit, the perfect combination of passion and persistence. Even if you have natural talent, it's nothing without grit.\nDuckworth says grit is the difference between success and failure. A person who has grit is more likely to succeed than a person who does not. When we think about attaining success—whether it's landing that job or learning that new skill—our thoughts are immediately burdened by all the things we must first learn. If you want that new job, you have to learn the job skills, then the interview skills, then the dress part—and you must be perfect at all of them. Grit is different because it tells us that perfection isn't the goal.\nGrit lifts the unreasonable expectations off our shoulders. Grit tells us that the door is open wider than we first thought possible. Grit allows us to redefine our goals. Think about it: what's something you've always wanted to do, but gave up because you \"don't have the skills for it\"? What's something you love but aren't good at?\nThe real workings of grit are to have sustainable passion and continue to try. Effort means more than your natural ability. Even if you haven't mastered a skill, grit tells you that you can still succeed if you can transform your passion into action. In a way, Duckworth is giving new hope to people who have shut the doors on their dreams. She is saying it is possible that you can accomplish anything. If at first you fail, then try one more time with grit.", "55": "With those born with natural talents, it feels as if they excel without really trying. But what about those of us who don't have a natural talent? We've been told all our lives that if you work hard, you too can succeed. But with the release of Angela Duckworth's Grit, we are given a new key to success.\n\"As much as talent counts, effort counts twice,\" says Duckworth in Grit. She introduces a new concept that talent may be overrated, and if you want real success, what you need is grit, the perfect combination of passion and persistence. Even if you have natural talent, it's nothing without grit.\nDuckworth says grit is the difference between success and failure. A person who has grit is more likely to succeed than a person who does not. When we think about attaining success—whether it's landing that job or learning that new skill—our thoughts are immediately burdened by all the things we must first learn. If you want that new job, you have to learn the job skills, then the interview skills, then the dress part—and you must be perfect at all of them. Grit is different because it tells us that perfection isn't the goal.\nGrit lifts the unreasonable expectations off our shoulders. Grit tells us that the door is open wider than we first thought possible. Grit allows us to redefine our goals. Think about it: what's something you've always wanted to do, but gave up because you \"don't have the skills for it\"? What's something you love but aren't good at?\nThe real workings of grit are to have sustainable passion and continue to try. Effort means more than your natural ability. Even if you haven't mastered a skill, grit tells you that you can still succeed if you can transform your passion into action. In a way, Duckworth is giving new hope to people who have shut the doors on their dreams. She is saying it is possible that you can accomplish anything. If at first you fail, then try one more time with grit."}, "questions": [{"id": "2025-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "How did Tariq make corn taste better?", "options": ["A) By slapping some butter on it.", "B) By enhancing its nourishment.", "C) By growing it in South Dakota.", "D) By cooking it in vegetable oil."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） By slapping some butter on it.\n【定位】Everything changed for 7-year-old Tariq when he slapped some butter on an ear of corn. He\nknew it tasted good, but butter made it better.\n对 7 岁的塔里克来说，当他往一根玉米上抹了点黄油时，一切都变了。他本就知道玉米好\n吃，但黄油让它更好吃了。\n【信号】but 后点出\"黄油让它更好吃\"。答案句的信号是转折词 but：but butter made it better 直\n接把 butter 和 made it better 挂钩，正好回答\"怎么让玉米更好吃\"，转折之后的这半句才\n是落点。\n【替换】he slapped some butter on an ear of corn ↔ By slapping some butter on it.\nan ear of corn 被代词 it 概括\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音第2段确实提到玉米能 provides nourishment across the globe，B 项 By enhancing\nits nourishment. 盗用了 nourishment 这个原词，但那是讲玉米向全球提供营养，跟\"塔里\n克怎么让玉米更好吃\"无关，听到 nourishment 就选就中陷阱。\n借用原文：The corn provides nourishment across the globe.\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 By growing it in South Dakota. 盗用了录音里的 South Dakota 这个高频地名，\nSouth Dakota is one of the top corn producers in America 说的是它是产玉米大州，\n与\"让玉米更好吃\"是两码事，属听到地名就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：South Dakota is one of the top corn producers in America.\nD) 未提及\nD 项 By cooking it in vegetable oil. 听起来也是个合理做法，且和正确项 A 的\"加黄油\"形\n成对立诱惑，但录音从头到尾只提了抹黄油，没说用植物油烹饪，属凭常识硬造、原文未提\n及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "Why were Tariq and his family invited to South Dakota?", "options": ["A) To introduce their corn to tourists.", "B) To attend an honorary ceremony.", "C) To share experience with other corn growers.", "D) To exhibit their corn at the state's Corn Palace."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） To attend an honorary ceremony.\n【定位】Tariq and his family were invited to South Dakota to attend the honorary ceremony at the\nstate's Corn Palace.\n塔里克和他的家人受邀前往南达科他，去参加该州玉米宫举行的荣誉典礼。\n【信号】invited to… to attend… 把人和目的连起来。答案信号是 invited 后接的目的不定式：\nwere invited to South Dakota to attend the honorary ceremony，to attend 直接说明\n受邀目的就是参加荣誉典礼，这句一字对上题干所问。\n【替换】to attend the honorary ceremony ↔ To attend an honorary ceremony.\nthe→an，去掉地点状语，核心动作不变\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音确实提到官方想突出 tourism and agriculture，A 项 To introduce their corn to\ntourists. 盗用了 tourism 引申出的 tourists，但那是官方的产业意图，不是塔里克一家\"被\n邀请\"的目的，二者主体不同，听到游客相关词就选会被带偏。\n借用原文：Officials wanted to highlight South Dakota's two largest industries: tourism\nand agriculture.\nC) 未提及\nC 项 To share experience with other corn growers. 借 corn 这个全篇高频词造出\"和其他\n玉米种植者交流经验\"的合理场景，但录音通篇没说塔里克要去交流经验，属凭话题臆造、\n未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 To exhibit their corn at the state's Corn Palace. 盗用了答案句里真实出现的地名 at\nthe state's Corn Palace，极具迷惑性；但录音说的是去玉米宫\"参加典礼\"，不是去那里\"展\n出他们的玉米\"，偷换了在玉米宫做的事，听到 Corn Palace 就选会中招。\n借用原文：to attend the honorary ceremony at the state's Corn Palace\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Two arrests were announced Thursday. The arrests were in connection with a string of mail thefts\nfrom U.S. Postal Service's collection boxes in Marion County and for the possession of a stolen postal\nkey, according to the Department of Justice.\n周四宣布了两起逮捕行动。据司法部称,这些逮捕与Marion County美国邮政管理局收集箱的一系列邮件\n盗窃案以及持有被盗邮政钥匙有关。\n2 Jordan Jax and Tarod Goodman, both 23, were arrested after evidence gathered in an investigation\nshowed the pair stole mail on multiple occasions using a postal key, according to a news release.\n据新闻稿称,23岁的Jordan Jax和Tarod Goodman在调查中收集的证据显示两人多次使用邮政钥匙盗窃邮\n件后被逮捕。\n3 Jax and Goodman stole mail for around four months. Investigators reviewed videos of the collection\nboxes; the video showed the two men loading the stolen mail into Jax's vehicle. Investigators were also\nable to recover the key that was used to open the collection boxes.\nJax和Goodman盗窃邮件约四个月。调查人员查看了收集箱的监控视频;视频显示两名男子将被盗邮件装\n入Jax的车辆。调查人员还找回了用于打开收集箱的钥匙。\n4 Jax and Goodman each face up to five years in federal prison in the mail thefts and up to ten years\nfor possession of the postal key.\nJax和Goodman各自面临邮件盗窃罪最高五年联邦监禁,以及持有邮政钥匙罪最高十年监禁。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What did Jordan Jax and Tarod Goodman do according to a news release?", "options": ["A) Stole mail several times.", "B) Forged postal keys illegally.", "C) Attacked postmen on multiple occasions.", "D) Broke a number of postal collection boxes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Stole mail several times.\n【定位】Jordan Jax and Tarod Goodman, both 23, were arrested after evidence gathered in an\ninvestigation showed the pair stole mail on multiple occasions using a postal key, according\nto a news release.\n据一份新闻稿，乔丹·贾克斯和塔罗德·古德曼，两人均为 23 岁，在一项调查所收集的证据显\n示二人多次盗窃邮件、并使用了一把邮政钥匙后，被逮捕。\n【信号】题干来源词回扣定位。题干里的 according to a news release 与录音 according to a\nnews release 逐字对应，这处来源短语就是定位信号：它出现的那一句正是承载答案的句\n子，紧接的 stole mail on multiple occasions 即两人所做之事。\n【替换】stole mail on multiple occasions ↔ Stole mail several times.\n多次的同义改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说两人 using a postal key，是使用一把邮政钥匙作案，并非 Forged postal keys\nillegally. 所说的非法伪造钥匙；选项盗用了 postal key 这一原词，却把使用偷换成伪造，\n答非所问。\n借用原文：using a postal key\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音里的 on multiple occasions 修饰的是盗窃邮件，Attacked postmen on multiple\noccasions. 却把这个多次短语套到攻击邮递员上；录音通篇没有任何袭击邮差的内容，属盗\n用频次短语的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：stole mail on multiple occasions\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音多处提到 collection boxes，如 collection boxes，但说的是两人用钥匙打开邮筒取\n信，从无砸坏之意；Broke a number of postal collection boxes. 盗用 collection boxes\n原词，硬加上 Broke 这一录音没有的动作。\n借用原文：collection boxes", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What do Jordan Jax and Tarod Goodman face?", "options": ["A) A sentence for life.", "B) Loss of all their possessions.", "C) Twenty-three years' hard labor.", "D) Up to fifteen years in prison."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Up to fifteen years in prison.\n【定位】Jax and Goodman each face up to five years in federal prison in the mail thefts and up to ten\nyears for possession of the postal key.\n贾克斯和古德曼二人因盗窃邮件各面临最高五年的联邦监禁，并因持有邮政钥匙各面临最高\n十年监禁。\n【信号】题干动词 face 原句重现。题干的 face 在录音 each face up to five years 里原样念出，这\n处动词回扣即定位信号；该句把两项刑期 up to five years 与 up to ten years 一并给出，\n正是答案所在。\n【替换】up to five years in federal prison ↔ Up to fifteen years in prison.\n两项刑期相加得十五年\nup to ten years for possession of the postal key ↔ Up to fifteen years in prison.\n另一项刑期并入总数\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音只说面临有期的 five years 与 ten years，从未提无期；A sentence for life. 是夸大到\n终身监禁的后果，录音并无对应内容。\nB) 未提及\n录音谈的全是坐牢的年限，未提任何财产损失；Loss of all their possessions. 所说丧失全\n部财产在录音里毫无依据。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里的 23 出自两人年龄 both 23，并非刑期；Twenty-three years' hard labor. 盗用 23\n这个数字，硬安成二十三年苦役，且 hard labor 录音也从未提及，把年龄数偷换成刑期。\n借用原文：both 23", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "Why has the clothing industry come under attack?", "options": ["A) It escapes regulation and misleads consumers.", "B) It ignores economically feasible recycling options.", "C) It creates waste and severely impacts the environment.", "D) It produces clothes affordable only to a tiny minority."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It creates waste and severely impacts the environment.\n【定位】Recently, the clothing industry has come under attack for creating a waste problem with\nserious environmental consequences.\n最近，服装业因制造废物问题、带来严重环境后果而受到抨击。\n【信号】题干原词 come under attack 复现处。录音中 has come under attack for 与题干 come\nunder attack 几乎一字不差，听到这个短语就锁定这句；紧跟 for 后面的内容正是被抨击的\n原因，即答案所在。\n【替换】creating a waste problem ↔ It creates waste\n动名词转一般现在时\nserious environmental consequences ↔ severely impacts the environment\n名词短语改写成动词短语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 misleads consumers（误导消费者），其中 consumers 是录音后文反复出现的原\n词，听到就想选；但录音说行业被抨击的原因是制造废物、害环境，从没说它误导消费者，\nA 借原词制造错觉。\n借用原文：incentivize both manufacturers and consumers\nB) 偷换概念\nB 说 ignores economically feasible recycling options（忽视经济可行的回收方案），其\n中 recycling、economically feasible 直接来自录音 environmentally and economically\nfeasible recycling options；但录音说的是这类回收方案本就缺失（in the absence of），\n不是行业故意忽视，B 把客观缺失偷换成主观无视。\n借用原文：In the absence of environmentally and economically feasible recycling options\nD) 与原文相反\nD 说 produces clothes affordable only to a tiny minority（生产只有极少数人买得起的\n衣服），clothes、produces 是录音原词，但录音明说快时尚是 low-quality clothes\nproduced in high volumes（低质、量产），价格便宜、人人能买，与 D 说的只有极少数\n人买得起恰好相反，录音也没提价格门槛。\n借用原文：low-quality clothes produced in high volumes", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "Why did the researchers put forth such policies as production tax?", "options": ["A) To cut consumers' expenses in buying quality clothes.", "B) To enable the whole fast-fashion industry to be sustainable.", "C) To incentivize manufacturers to be more consumer-friendly.", "D) To urge manufacturers and consumers to reduce waste."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） To urge manufacturers and consumers to reduce waste.\n【定位】The researchers put forth policy contributions, ranging from sustainable disposal of leftover\nstock to production tax to incentivize both manufacturers and consumers to be more\nwaste conscious.\n研究者提出了一系列政策建议，从对剩余库存的可持续处理到征收生产税，目的是激励生产\n商和消费者都更有减废意识。\n【信号】目的状语 to incentivize。录音里 to incentivize both manufacturers and consumers to\nbe more waste conscious 是 production tax 后面的目的状语，to incentivize 就是表目\n的的信号，它后面的内容正是这些政策要达成的目标。\n【替换】incentivize both manufacturers and consumers ↔ urge manufacturers and consumers\n同义动词替换\nto be more waste conscious ↔ to reduce waste\n概括式改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 cut consumers' expenses in buying quality clothes（替消费者省下买好衣服的\n钱），consumers 是录音原词，但录音讲政策目的是减废，从没说为消费者省钱，A 把减\n废目的偷换成省钱目的。\n借用原文：incentivize both manufacturers and consumers\nB) 过度推断\nB 说 To enable the whole fast-fashion industry to be sustainable（让整个快时尚业可\n持续），sustainable 是录音开头出现的原词，但生产税这类政策的直接目的录音说得很具\n体——是激励双方减废，而非让整个行业可持续，B 把目的范围夸大了。\n借用原文：Can fast-fashion be sustainable?\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说 incentivize manufacturers to be more consumer-friendly（激励生产商更体贴消费\n者），incentivize、manufacturers 是录音原词，但录音说的是激励双方更有减废意识，不\n是让生产商对消费者更友好，C 把减废目的偷换成讨好消费者。\n借用原文：incentivize both manufacturers and consumers to be more waste conscious", "points": ["听力·长对话", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What should be done first to eliminate the harm caused by the clothing industry?", "options": ["A) Recognizing the impact on consumers.", "B) Identifying the source of the problem.", "C) Abandoning the current business model.", "D) Establishing powerful regulatory bodies."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Identifying the source of the problem.\n【定位】In order to devise effective policies to curb the environmental impact of the clothing industry,\nit is important to identify the source of the problem in the supply chain.\n为了制定有效政策来遏制服装业对环境的影响，关键是先找出供应链中问题的根源。\n【信号】强调句 it is important to。录音 it is important to identify the source of the problem\n用 it is important to 强调首要之事，这就是表先后、表首要的信号词；它后面的动作\nidentify the source 即题目问的第一步。\n【替换】identify the source of the problem ↔ Identifying the source of the problem\n动词原形转动名词，近乎原句\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 Recognizing the impact on consumers，其中 recognize、impact 来自录音\nrecognize the environmental impact of fast-fashion，听到就想选；但录音把这一步放\n在找出根源之后，而且说的是认识环境影响、不是对消费者的影响，既不是第一步、对象也\n被改了。\n借用原文：recognize the environmental impact of fast-fashion\nC) 过度推断\nC 说 Abandoning the current business model（放弃现有商业模式），business model\n取自录音 create new business models，但录音说的是创造新商业模式，从没说要放弃现\n有模式，C 把创造夸张成放弃。\n借用原文：create new business models to manage waste\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 Establishing powerful regulatory bodies（建立强有力的监管机构），regulatory\nbodies 出现在录音 Manufacturers, consumers, and regulatory bodies 中，但录音只是\n提到监管机构是参与方之一，从没说要先建立强有力的监管机构，D 是凭原词编出来的。\n借用原文：Manufacturers, consumers, and regulatory bodies can then take an informed approach", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why is the woman complaining?", "options": ["A) The city's rush-hour traffic is intolerable.", "B) She cannot avoid rush-hour traffic.", "C) The local government is inefficient.", "D) The city is too big to move around easily."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The city's rush-hour traffic is intolerable.\n【定位】Rush hour in this city is killing me.\n这座城市的高峰期真要把我折腾死了。\n【信号】她开口的第一句直接吐槽。男方问\"你怎么这么烦/出什么事了\"之后，女方应答的第一句\nRush hour in this city is killing me. 就是直接吐槽，is killing me 这种强烈情绪用语正是\n抱怨的落点信号——被问者的第一反应往往就是答案句。\n【替换】Rush hour in this city is killing me. ↔ The city's rush-hour traffic is intolerable.\nis killing me 改写成 intolerable\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 盗用了录音里反复出现的 Rush hour 来勾人，但她抱怨的是高峰期\"让人受不了\"，从没\n说\"她躲不开高峰\"。She cannot avoid rush-hour traffic. 是凭空加上的内容，属听到原词\n就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：Rush hour is terrible\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后面女方确实提到 the local government could help improve things，C 盗用\"政\n府\"这个原词，却编成\"政府效率低\"。她说的是\"政府本可以帮忙改善\"，并没说政府无能，C\n答非所问。\n借用原文：the local government could help improve things\nD) 原词陷阱\n男方说过 a city this big，D 盗用\"城市大\"这个原词，编成\"城市太大不好出行\"。但那是男\n方的话，且女方抱怨的对象是高峰期本身，不是城市大小，D 既张冠李戴又答非所问。\n借用原文：a city this big", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the woman suggest doing?", "options": ["A) Upgrading subway carriages.", "B) Increasing the number of bus routes.", "C) Air conditioning the city's subway.", "D) Learning from other big cities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Air conditioning the city's subway.\n【定位】Well, for example, the subway system could have air conditioning. I know many cities in the\nworld have air conditioning in their subway, so why can't we?\n比如说，地铁系统可以装空调嘛。我知道世界上很多城市的地铁都有空调，咱们为什么不能\n有呢？\n【信号】建议信号 could 与 why can't we。the subway system could have air conditioning 里\n的 could（可以…）就是建议信号，紧接着 why can't we（咱为什么不能）用反问加重了这\n条提议——could + 反问，正是\"我建议这么做\"的落点。\n【替换】the subway system could have air conditioning ↔ Air conditioning the city's subway.\n句子改写成动名词短语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 盗用录音里的 subway carriages（地铁车厢）来勾人。\"车厢\"确实念到了，但女方的建\n议是\"装空调\"，不是\"升级车厢\"。况且后面 The trains here are too old 是男方说的，不是\n女方的建议，A 答非所问。\n借用原文：strangers packed close together in the subway carriages\nB) 未提及\n增开公交线路这件事女方从没提过。录音只在男方那段提到 Bus No. 36，与\"增加线路\"无\n关，B 属凭空捏造的措施。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 盗用了 many cities in the world 里的\"别的城市\"来勾人，但女方提\"很多城市有空调\"是\n为论证\"应给地铁装空调\"，并非建议\"向别的大城市学习\"。D 把具体建议偷换成空泛口号，\n答非所问。\n借用原文：I know many cities in the world have air conditioning in their subway", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do we learn about the man from the conversation?", "options": ["A) He suffers from rush-hour crowding too.", "B) He is fortunate to live very near to work.", "C) He takes a half-hour bus ride to work.", "D) He is going to move closer to his office."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He takes a half-hour bus ride to work.\n【定位】Yeah, it's more convenient. Bus No. 36 goes straight from my house to the office. It's a 30-\nminute ride and I don't have to make any changes.\n对，公交更方便。36 路车从我家直达办公室，车程 30 分钟，中途不用换乘。\n【信号】男方自述上班方式 + 数字 30-minute。Bus No. 36 goes straight from my house to the\noffice 是男方在讲自己怎么上班，紧跟的 It's a 30-minute ride 给出具体时长——\"自述 +\n数字\"是细节题最硬的落点信号，听到数字就要立刻记下。\n【替换】It's a 30-minute ride ↔ He takes a half-hour bus ride to work.\n30-minute 换说成 half-hour\nBus No. 36 goes straight from my house to the office ↔ bus ride to work\n坐公交去办公室=坐公交上班\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nA 盗用录音里 rush-hour crowds 这个\"高峰拥挤\"原词来勾人，但受高峰挤之苦的是女方\n（坐地铁），男方说自己坐公交、还自称 fortunate。把女方的处境安到男方身上，是张冠\n李戴。\n借用原文：the rush-hour crowds with strangers packed close together\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 盗用男方说的 I'm fortunate（我算幸运）来勾人。他幸运是因为\"坐公交方便、不用挤地\n铁\"，而不是\"住得离公司很近\"——通勤还要 30 分钟，谈不上很近。B 把\"幸运的原因\"偷换\n了。\n借用原文：I guess I'm fortunate I take the bus instead\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 盗用了 move closer to the office 这个原词，但说\"也许我该搬得离公司近点\"的是女\n方，不是男方。把女方的打算记到男方头上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Maybe I should move closer to the office", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the woman ask the man to do at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Recommend to her a good house near his office.", "B) Send her his housing agent's telephone number.", "C) Tell her exactly what kind of property to look for.", "D) Make sure the agent finds something good for her."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Send her his housing agent's telephone number.\n【定位】Hmm, could you send me his number, please?\n嗯，你能把他的电话号码发给我吗？\n【信号】请求句 could you…please。could you send me his number, please? 用 could you…\nplease 提出请求，正是\"请对方做某事\"的标准信号，且出现在对话末尾，与题干\"at the\nend\"对得上——这就是答案句。\n【替换】could you send me his number, please? ↔ Send her his housing agent's telephone number.\nhis number 补全为中介的电话号码\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 盗用了对话里 move closer to the office、flat 这一带\"房子\"话题来勾人，但她请的是\"发\n中介电话\"，不是\"请他直接推荐一套好房子\"。把\"给电话\"偷换成\"荐房\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：I found the flat I'm living in now through him\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 盗用了男方那句 tell him exactly what you are looking for，但那是男方让\"她去告诉中\n介\"要什么，不是她请男方做的事，也不是\"由男方告诉她该找什么样的房\"。既张冠李戴又把\n方向弄反。\n借用原文：Just tell him exactly what you are looking for\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 盗用男方那句 he will find something good 来勾人，但那是男方对中介能办好的预期，\n不是女方请男方\"确保中介给她找到好的\"。把一句预期偷换成她的请求，答非所问。\n借用原文：I'm sure he will find something good", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the man say about Johnny?", "options": ["A) He was remembered for the scar on his cheek.", "B) He received a heart operation a few days ago.", "C) He used to wear a sad face in school.", "D) He had some plastic surgery done."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He had some plastic surgery done.\n【定位】He had some plastic surgery done.\n他做了整形手术。\n【信号】对方一开口的核心信息。男生在被问『他还好吗』之后，第一句给出的实质消息就是 He\nhad some plastic surgery done.，这是整段对话的引子和核心事件，承载本题答案。\n【替换】He had some plastic surgery done. ↔ He had some plastic surgery done.\n几乎原句照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 He was remembered for the scar on his cheek. 借了录音里真出现过的 scar 和\ncheek（女生说 I always remember him having that on his cheek），但那是女生说『记\n得他脸上有疤』，并非男生关于 Johnny 所说的核心事件；题问 the man 说了什么，答非\n所问。\n借用原文：I always remember him having that on his cheek\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 He received a heart operation a few days ago. 蹭了录音里的 operation 一词（He\nhad an operation done on it, plastic surgery），却擅自改成 heart operation（心脏手\n术）；录音说的是脸上的整形手术，不是心脏，属偷换。\n借用原文：He had an operation done on it, plastic surgery\nC) 未提及\n选项 He used to wear a sad face in school. 里的 sad face（哭丧脸）录音根本没说，只\n是借了 school 一词（女生提到 I've known him since primary school）凑出来的；属无\n中生有。\n借用原文：I've known him since primary school", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the woman find puzzling?", "options": ["A) The man's deep sympathy for Johnny.", "B) The man's use of the expression 'fixed'.", "C) The man's covering up of what happened.", "D) The man's good memory of his childhood."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） The man's use of the expression 'fixed'.\n【定位】Fixed? What do you mean? You can't fix a scar.\n修好？你什么意思？疤是没法『修好』的。\n【信号】对某个用词的反问。女生紧接男生说的 he had it fixed，立刻反问 Fixed? What do you\nmean? You can't fix a scar.——她重复并质疑了 fixed 这个词，反问语气正是『困惑』的\n信号，答案句就在此。\n【替换】Fixed? What do you mean? You can't fix a scar. ↔ The man's use of the expression 'fixed'.\n把疑问句概括为『对用词不解』\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 The man's deep sympathy for Johnny. 说男生对 Johnny 深表同情，录音里男生从\n头到尾没有表达同情，更不是女生困惑的点；属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 The man's covering up of what happened. 蹭了录音里 in order to cover it and\nmake it less noticeable 的 cover，但原文 cover 是『遮盖那道疤』，选项偷换成『掩盖发\n生的事（隐瞒）』，且女生并不为此困惑。\n借用原文：in order to cover it and make it less noticeable\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 The man's good memory of his childhood. 里『好记性』其实是女生自己说的（I\nalways remember him having that on his cheek），却被安到 the man 头上，且与『困\n惑』无关；属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I always remember him having that on his cheek", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What did the man think of Johnny's scar?", "options": ["A) It gave him a noticeable smartness.", "B) It made him appear even more manly.", "C) It seemed to give him a certain uniqueness.", "D) It seemed to make up for his physical defects."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） It seemed to give him a certain uniqueness.\n【定位】In fact, I thought it added character… like it gave him a certain uniqueness.\n其实我觉得那道疤还添了几分个性……好像让他有种独特感。\n【信号】I thought 引出的主观评价。男生用 In fact, I thought it added character… like it gave\nhim a certain uniqueness，I thought 直接亮出他对疤的看法，且 In fact 加重了肯定语\n气，是承载态度的答案句。\n【替换】it gave him a certain uniqueness ↔ It seemed to give him a certain uniqueness.\n近乎同句，只换了语气词\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 It gave him a noticeable smartness. 蹭了录音里多次出现的 noticeable（make it\nless noticeable），但 smartness（精明/帅气）男生从没说过；属用熟词拼凑的原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：make it less noticeable\nB) 未提及\n选项 It made him appear even more manly. 说疤让他更有男子气概，录音里男生评价用\n的是 character、uniqueness，从未说 manly；属未提及。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 It seemed to make up for his physical defects. 蹭了录音 physical defects 一词，\n但男生说的是『有些缺陷确实丑、可 Johnny 的疤不是』，并不认为疤在弥补缺陷；这是反\n着用了原文，张冠李戴加曲解。\n借用原文：I can understand some scars and physical defects can be ugly and distracting, but\nnot Johnny's", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "How could we describe the speakers' attitude towards Johnny's girlfriend?", "options": ["A) Liberal.", "B) Hostile.", "C) Indifferent.", "D) Critical."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） Critical.\n【定位】What? That's so superficial and selfish of her. If Johnny's girlfriend doesn't like him for who\nhe is, then she does not deserve to be his girlfriend.\n什么？她这么做也太肤浅、太自私了。如果 Johnny 的女友不能接受真实的他，那她就不配\n做他女友。\n【信号】连串贬义评价词。女生对女友连用 so superficial and selfish（肤浅、自私）和 does not\ndeserve（不配），都是强烈负面用词；加上男生附和 You are right，两人态度一致偏负\n面，这几句是态度答案句。\n【替换】That's so superficial and selfish of her ↔ Critical.\n贬义评价归为『批评』\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 Liberal.（开明、宽容）方向与录音相反——两人对女友是 superficial and selfish 的\n指责，毫无宽容之意；属与原文相反。\n借用原文：That's so superficial and selfish of her\nB) 过度推断\n选项 Hostile.（充满敌意）程度太重，录音是批评女友的做法（肤浅、自私），并未到仇\n视、敌对的地步；属程度过度推断。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 Indifferent.（漠不关心）与录音情绪冲突——女生说到激动处骂女友、还评 What a\nfool he is，明显在意、不是无所谓；属与原文相反。\n借用原文：What a fool he is", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "How did language come into being according to researchers?", "options": ["A) Through deliberation.", "B) Through evolution.", "C) Through invention.", "D) Through collaboration."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Through evolution.\n【定位】Researchers don't think language was invented; instead, they think it evolved.\n研究者不认为语言是被发明出来的；相反，他们认为它是进化（演变）而来的。\n【信号】否定+instead 的对比落点。这句靠 don't think 先否掉一个说法、再用 instead 引出真正\n的答案——『否定 + instead/but』之后才是真意。前半 language was invented（被发\n明）是要被排除的，后半 they think it evolved（进化而来）才是研究者的真实立场。\n【替换】they think it evolved ↔ Through evolution.\n动词 evolved 名词化为 evolution\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nThrough invention. 直接套用了录音里的 invented 一词，听到 invent 就想选正中陷阱\n——但原文是 don't think language was invented，明确否定了『发明』这个说法，所以\n它恰恰是错的。\n借用原文：Researchers don't think language was invented\nA) 未提及\nThrough deliberation.（通过深思熟虑/商议）在录音里根本没出现，研究者只在 invented\n和 evolved 之间表态，从未说语言是『商量出来的』。\nD) 未提及\nThrough collaboration.（通过协作）听上去合理，但录音只谈『进化还是发明』，从没说\n语言是靠人们协作产生的，属于自己脑补、原文未给的内容。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "How did language start according to the first theory?", "options": ["A) With people imitating sounds around them.", "B) With people interacting with animals close by.", "C) With people showing each other things nearby.", "D) With people trying to string sounds together."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） With people imitating sounds around them.\n【定位】The first theory is that language started with people making different sounds, mostly imitating\nthe things around them, like animal calls, nature sounds, and the sounds of tools.\n第一个理论是：语言始于人们发出各种不同的声音，主要是模仿周围的事物，比如动物的叫\n声、自然的声响和工具发出的声音。\n【信号】started with 直接点出起点。这句用 language started with（语言始于……）这种明确的\n『起点』标志词，正面回答题干问的『怎么开始』；其后的 imitating the things around\nthem（模仿周围事物）就是起步的具体动作。\n【替换】making different sounds, mostly imitating the things around them ↔\nWith people imitating sounds around them.\nimitating things around→imitating sounds around\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nWith people interacting with animals close by. 抓住了录音里的 animal calls（动物叫\n声）做诱饵，但原文说的是『模仿动物的叫声』，不是『和身边的动物互动』——听到\nanimal 就选就掉进陷阱，模仿声音 ≠ 与动物互动。\n借用原文：like animal calls, nature sounds, and the sounds of tools\nD) 答非所问\nWith people trying to string sounds together. 套用了原文 stringing the words\ntogether，但那是录音明说『At some point』（在某个时间点）才出现的后期步骤，不是\n『怎么开始』；它答的是后来的事，与题干问的『起点』错位。\n借用原文：people started stringing the words together to form sentences\nC) 张冠李戴\nWith people showing each other things nearby. 的『互相展示身边的东西』其实更接近\n第三段第二个理论里的『比划、用手指东西』，而本题限定 the first theory，张冠李戴把\n另一个理论的内容搬了过来。\n借用原文：pointing at things with their hands and imitating actions using their bodies", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "Why did people switch from gesturing to making sounds according to the more recent theory?", "options": ["A) Making sounds can free one's hands for doing other things.", "B) Making sounds proves markedly easier than sign languages.", "C) Making sounds enables one to express their ideas more explicitly.", "D) Making sounds helps one communicate with people they can't see."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Making sounds helps one communicate with people they can't see.\n【定位】The reason they switched to making sounds, the theory goes, is that talking out loud lets you\ncommunicate with someone even when you can't see them.\n按这个理论的说法，人们之所以改用发声，原因是大声说话能让你即使在看不见对方的时候\n也能与之交流。\n【信号】The reason…is that 直给原因。这句以 The reason they switched to making sounds\n开头、用 is that 直接引出理由，是最明确的因果信号；is that 后面的 even when you\ncan't see them 就是『为什么改成出声』的答案。\n【替换】communicate with someone even when you can't see them ↔\nMaking sounds helps one communicate with people they can't see.\n几乎逐词对应的同义改写\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nMaking sounds can free one's hands for doing other things.『腾出双手做别的事』听\n上去很合理，但录音从手语切到发声时只给了『看不见也能交流』这一个理由，从没提过\n『腾出手』，这是凭常识自己补出来的，原文未给。\nB) 原词陷阱\nMaking sounds proves markedly easier than sign languages. 借用了录音里的 sign\nlanguage，但原文只说手语后来发展出发声，从没比较『出声比手语更容易』，这个\neasier 的比较是凭空加上去的。\n借用原文：after developing sign languages, people eventually started making sounds\nC) 未提及\nMaking sounds enables one to express their ideas more explicitly.『能更清楚地表达想\n法』是个看似合理的好处，但录音给出的唯一理由是『看不见对方也能交流』，从没说出声\n能表达得『更明确』，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "How would people describe someone who shows off their social status according to new research?", "options": ["A) Well disciplined.", "B) Quite confident.", "C) Somewhat selfish.", "D) Highly intelligent."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度观点题） Somewhat selfish.\n【定位】But new research found that people believe someone who shows off their social status cares\nmore about benefiting themselves than helping others.\n但新研究发现，人们认为炫耀自己社会地位的人更在意让自己得益，而非帮助他人。\n【信号】转折 But + new research。答案句以 But new research found 起头，转折词 But 加上题\n干点名的『new research』双重信号——前一句讲『显得有钱被认为更聪明自律』是铺\n垫，But 之后才是新研究的真正结论，这正是答案落点。\n【替换】cares more about benefiting themselves than helping others ↔ Somewhat selfish.\n整句释义→一词概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nWell disciplined. 盗用了答案句前那句铺垫里的 disciplined，但那句说的是『显得有钱的\n人被认为更自律』，是 But 之前的对比信息，不是新研究对炫耀者的评价，听到\ndisciplined 就选正中陷阱。\n借用原文：people who appear to be wealthy tend to be considered more intelligent, disciplined,\nand competent\nB) 未提及\nQuite confident.『相当自信』录音通篇没有提到，是凭『炫耀的人大概很自信』的常识硬\n推出来的，没有原文依据。\nD) 原词陷阱\nHighly intelligent. 盗用了铺垫句里的 intelligent，同样出自 But 之前『显得有钱者被认为\n更聪明』的对比信息，不是新研究对炫耀者的真正评价，与 A 是同一类陷阱。\n借用原文：people who appear to be wealthy tend to be considered more intelligent", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "When were participants more likely to choose someone who signaled their wealth or status?", "options": ["A) When they wanted to have a competitive team member.", "B) When they were looking for a cooperative workmate.", "C) When they found collaboration essential to them.", "D) When they were seeking someone to help them."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） When they wanted to have a competitive team member.\n【定位】But participants were more likely to choose that person when they're looking for a\ncompetitive team member.\n但当参与者在寻找一名有竞争力的团队成员时，他们更倾向于选这个人。\n【信号】转折 But + more likely。本段先说『seeking cooperative members 时不太会选』，But\nparticipants were more likely to choose 用 But 转折并出现题干同款的 more likely to\nchoose，这句把『更愿意选』明确配给了后面那种情形，正是答案句。\n【替换】looking for a competitive team member ↔\nWhen they wanted to have a competitive team member.\n近义动词替换\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nWhen they were looking for a cooperative workmate. 盗用了 looking for 的句式和\n『合作』概念，但录音说『找 cooperative members 时反而 less likely 选』，正好与题干\n问的 more likely 相反，是把『不选』的情形错当成『选』的情形。\n借用原文：participants were less likely to choose someone who signals their wealth or status to\njoin a group seeking cooperative members\nC) 原词陷阱\nWhen they found collaboration essential to them. 搬来第一段的 cooperation is\nessential 那层意思（collaboration、essential），听着很耳熟，但那是讲『需要合作时该\n谦逊』，与本题『更愿意选炫耀者』的情形无关，是典型听到熟词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：Modesty may be key when cooperation is essential\nD) 未提及\nWhen they were seeking someone to help them.『想找人帮忙时』录音从未把『更愿意\n选炫耀者』绑到这种情形上，是凭常识臆造的条件，无原文依据。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What should people do when sharing their wealth and status online?", "options": ["A) Avoid frightening competitors.", "B) Make clear their social goal.", "C) Adopt persuasive strategies.", "D) Consider the consequences."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Consider the consequences.\n【定位】People can easily share their wealth and status to large audiences, but they need to consider\nthe consequences.\n人们可以轻松地向广大受众展示自己的财富和地位，但他们需要考虑后果。\n【信号】建议信号 need to + 转折 but。末段先说『社媒能轻易把财富地位分享给大众』，but\nthey need to consider the consequences 用 but 转折并带 need to（需要做某事）这一\n建议信号，把『该怎么做』直接点出，是答案句。\n【替换】need to consider the consequences ↔ Consider the consequences.\n建议句去主语即选项\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nAvoid frightening competitors. 搬来末段的 frighten competitors，但录音说炫耀『可以\n帮你吓退竞争者』，那是分享地位的好处之一，并非『应该做什么』的建议；而且选项加了\nAvoid（避免）把意思反成『避免吓退』，与原文方向不符。\n借用原文：Posting about luxury purchases and expensive vacations online may help you to\npersuade others and frighten competitors\nB) 张冠李戴\nMake clear their social goal. 借了第二段的 depending on their social goal，但那句讲\n的是『按社交目标调整自我展示』，不是末段『网上分享该怎么做』的建议，是把别段内容\n安到本题头上。\n借用原文：people should change how they present themselves depending on their social goal\nC) 原词陷阱\nAdopt persuasive strategies. 盗用末段 persuade others 的词根（persuasive），但录音\n说炫耀『可以帮你说服别人』也是好处之一，不是建议人去『采取说服策略』，属听到\npersuade 就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：may help you to persuade others", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What can we see in the oldest cave paintings?", "options": ["A) Much evidence of humans capturing animals.", "B) More images of animals than humans.", "C) Fewer images of buildings than animals.", "D) Little proof of human fascination with animals."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） More images of animals than humans.\n【定位】Of the oldest cave paintings discovered, some are up to 40,000 years old, and there are more\nimages of animals than humans.\n在已发现的最古老的洞穴壁画中，有些距今多达四万年，而且其中动物的图像比人类的图像\n更多。\n【信号】题干原词 oldest cave paintings 复现处。原文 Of the oldest cave paintings\ndiscovered 与题干 the oldest cave paintings 是同一处，紧跟其后的 there are more\nimages of animals than humans 就是承载答案的关键句，靠题干主题词在原文复现来\n定位。\n【替换】more images of animals than humans ↔ More images of animals than humans.\n几乎原句照搬\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nMuch evidence of humans capturing animals. 盗用了下一句 humans began to\ncapture and hold animals 的 capture 一词，但那讲的是后来人类开始圈养动物，不是壁\n画内容；题问壁画里能看到什么，答非所问。\n借用原文：humans began to capture and hold animals\nC) 未提及\nFewer images of buildings than animals. 里的 buildings 在第一段壁画的描述中根本没\n提（buildings 出现在第二段讲埃及建筑处），壁画只比较了 animals 与 humans，没说\nbuildings 的图像，属凭空捏造。\nD) 与原文相反\nLittle proof of human fascination with animals. 用了开头句 Human fascination with\nanimals 的原词，却说成 Little proof（很少证据）；而原文开头明说人类对动物的迷恋自\n古就有、壁画里动物图像还更多，恰恰是证据充分，极性完全相反。\n借用原文：Human fascination with animals goes back as far as humans do", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do we learn about the first-known collections of animals?", "options": ["A) They were open to the public.", "B) They were primarily native.", "C) They were kept by royalty.", "D) They were hardly injured."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They were kept by royalty.\n【定位】The first-known collections were held by royalty and were not open to the public.\n已知最早的（动物）收藏由王室持有，并且不对公众开放。\n【信号】题干主语 first-known collections 在原文复现处。原文 The first-known collections\nwere held by royalty 与题干 the first-known collections of animals 是同一主语，紧跟\n其后对「归谁所有、是否开放」的正面陈述就是答案句，靠题干主语在原文复现来定位。\n【替换】were held by royalty ↔ They were kept by royalty.\nheld 同义替换为 kept\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey were open to the public. 借用了原文 not open to the public 里的 open to the\npublic 短语，却把否定的 not 去掉，说成对公众开放；原文恰恰说这些最早的收藏不对公\n众开放，极性相反。\n借用原文：were not open to the public\nB) 与原文相反\nThey were primarily native. 盗用了原文 native 一词，却把意思整个说反：原文是 not\nnative to Egypt（大象不是埃及本土的），选项却说 primarily native（主要是本土的），\n方向相反。\n借用原文：which were not native to Egypt\nD) 与原文相反\nThey were hardly injured. 借用了原文 injuries 一词，却说几乎没受伤；原文明说 their\nremains show evidence of severe injuries（遗骸显示有严重伤害），是伤得很重，与选\n项正好相反。\n借用原文：their remains show evidence of severe injuries", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What might be the reason the Egyptian Queen created the zoo?", "options": ["A) To enable the public to watch animals close by.", "B) To introduce to Egyptians animals from Punt.", "C) To protect the endangered animals.", "D) To show off her riches and power."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） To show off her riches and power.\n【定位】It's unclear why the Queen built the zoo, but it might have been to show off her wealth\nand power.\n女王为何建这座动物园尚不清楚，但可能是为了炫耀她的财富与权力。\n【信号】转折 but 之后的目的句。答案句的信号是转折加目的：It's unclear why 先说原因不明，转\n折词 but 之后 it might have been to show off her wealth and power 才给出真正的可能\n原因，but 后 + to do 处即答案。\n【替换】to show off her wealth and power ↔ To show off her riches and power.\nwealth 同义替换为 riches\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo enable the public to watch animals close by. 把第三段开头 The first public exhibit\nof animals 里的 public 拿来当动机，但 public exhibit 只是给这座动物园定性，原文说的\n建园原因是炫耀财富权力，不是为让公众近距离看动物。\n借用原文：The first public exhibit of animals\nB) 张冠李戴\nTo introduce to Egyptians animals from Punt. 盗用了原文 a far-off land known as\nPunt 的地名 Punt，但原文说 Punt 只是动物的来源地（从那带回动物），不是建园是为了\n向埃及人介绍这些动物，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：animals brought home from a far-off land known as Punt\nC) 未提及\nTo protect the endangered animals. 说为保护濒危动物，但全文从未提到 protect 或\nendangered，建园原因原文只给了炫耀财富权力一种，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do we learn about King Henry I's collection of animals in England?", "options": ["A) It was part of the royal estate.", "B) It was a shelter for wild animals.", "C) It was the kingdom's best-equipped zoo.", "D) It was the first public exhibit of animals."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It was part of the royal estate.\n【定位】In England, King Henry I set up a collection of animals in about 1110 as part of the\nroyal estate.\n在英格兰，亨利一世国王约在 1110 年建立了一处动物收藏，作为王室领地的一部分。\n【信号】人名 King Henry I 后紧跟的定性。定位靠人名复现：题干问 King Henry I，原文 In\nEngland, King Henry I set up a collection of animals 一出现这个名字，紧跟的 as part\nof the royal estate 就是对这处收藏的定性，即答案句。\n【替换】as part of the royal estate ↔ It was part of the royal estate.\n近乎原句对应\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt was a shelter for wild animals. 借用了全篇反复出现的 animals，但原文从没把亨利一\n世这处收藏说成野生动物的庇护所（shelter），这是凭空添加的定性。\nC) 过度推断\nIt was the kingdom's best-equipped zoo. 用 best-equipped（装备最好的）拔高，但原\n文只说它是 part of the royal estate 并养有老虎狮子，从未评价它是王国里设施最好的动\n物园，属过度拔高。\n借用原文：His collection included tigers and lions\nD) 张冠李戴\nIt was the first public exhibit of animals. 盗用了第三段 The first public exhibit of\nanimals 的原话，但那说的是埃及女王 Hatshepsut 的动物园，不是英格兰亨利一世这处，\n把别处的定性安到了这处头上。\n借用原文：The first public exhibit of animals may have been created by Egypt's Queen Hatshepsut", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) complex\n形容词\n【词性槽】the kinds of 后接名词，空与 strategic 并列修饰名词\n【依据】与 strategic 同属「高级心智」语义场\nthe kinds of __(26)__ and strategic self-presentation behaviour we see in adults：作\n者强调成年人的自我呈现是「复杂的、有策略的」，complex（复杂的）与 strategic（策\n略性的）语义并列、相互呼应。\n【竞争词】K) solemn 感情色彩与上下文不符\nsolemn 意为「庄严的、肃穆的」，多用于仪式或表情，和「自我呈现行为」的认知性描述不搭\n边，也无法和 strategic 构成自然的并列。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) previously\n副词\n【词性槽】than 后修饰过去分词 known\n【依据】与 New findings 形成「新 vs 旧」对照\nNew findings suggest that ... appear at a much younger age than __(27)__ known. 强\n调「新发现」与「以往所知」对比，previously known = 此前所知，正好把「新发现 vs 旧\n认知」对照起来。\n【竞争词】M) suddenly 语义和逻辑都不通\nsuddenly known（突然被知道）既不是固定表达，也和「比以往认为更早」的对比语境不沾\n边，明显逻辑错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accepted\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词后接过去分词作被动谓语\n【依据】符合「被认同/接纳」的社会心理语境\nkids want to be __(28)__ by those they admire 谈孩子希望「被自己崇拜的人认可、接\n纳」，accepted by 是地道搭配，对应「获得社会认同」这个本段的主线。\n【竞争词】J) selected 选拔语义与「被仰慕者接纳」不匹配\nselected by 多指「被挑中」（比赛、选拔），带筛选意味，而孩子对「自己崇拜的人」的需求\n是被接纳、被认可，不是被挑选；语境不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) constitutes\n动词\n【词性槽】what 引导从句需要谓语动词\n【依据】what constitutes X = 何为 X 的构成\nlearn about what __(29)__ a desirable reputation 即「了解什么构成了好名声」，\nconstitute 在学术英语里恰好用作「构成、等同于」，是描述抽象概念边界的常用动词。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) building\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 for 后接动名词作宾语\n【依据】build a reputation 是固定搭配\nstrategies that are effective for __(30)__ a good reputation，build a good reputation\n（建立好名声）是英语里的高频固定搭配，对应「塑造形象」的本段主题。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) vary\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 will 后接动词原形\n【依据】vary one's behaviour = 改变/调整自己的行为\nThey will __(31)__ their behaviour in order to appear moral or socially good in the\neyes of key observers. 主题是「为博得好印象而调整行为」，vary 作及物动词意为\n「使……变化、改变」，vary their behaviour 正对应「策略性地改变行为」。\n【竞争词】N) understand 理解自己行为 ≠ 改变以塑形象\nunderstand their behaviour 表「理解自己的行为」，与 in order to appear moral（为了显\n得有道德）这个目的状语不构成因果——理解了并不一定就显得有道德。understand 此处被\n32 题预留，更不该提前用掉。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— understand their behaviour 看似合理，实则脱离目的状\n语。They will __(31)__ their behaviour in order to appear moral or socially good：\nunderstand 同为动词原形且能带宾语，但「理解自己的行为」无法导致「显得有道德」\n这个目的；vary 才符合「调整行为以塑形象」的逻辑。识破靠用 in order to 的目的反推\n动词。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) understand\n动词\n【词性槽】whether children ___ and use ... 谓语动词\n【依据】和 use 构成「理解并运用」的递进\nwhether children __(32)__ and use the fact that different attributes are valuable at\ndifferent times：understand and use a fact 是英语里「先理解、再运用」的常见递进，\n正对应「是否懂得并利用不同属性的价值」。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) audiences\n名词\n【词性槽】different 后 + to 介词宾语的复数名词\n【依据】与「印象管理对象」的语境直接对应\nadults use a large variety of attributes to manage and create impressions 与\ndifferent attributes are valuable at different times to different __(33)__ 谈的都是「在\n不同观察者面前营造印象」，audiences（受众/观众）正指「印象呈现给谁看」，逻辑严丝\n合缝。\n【竞争词】F) deputies 语义偏离「印象管理」主题\ndeputies 指「副手、代理人」，是政治/职务术语，与本文讨论的「印象的接收者」毫无关联。\nI) samples 样本不属于印象管理对象\nsamples（样本）多用于实验或商品场景，无法承担「在不同对象面前调整形象」里的「对象」\n角色。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— different samples 形式上完美，语义却偏题。different\nattributes are valuable at different times to different __(33)__：samples 是复数名词、\n与 different times 形式平行，但本段主题是「在不同观察者面前展示形象」，audiences\n才是真正的受众。识破靠回到段落主旨词 impressions/observers 锚定语义。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) struggle\n动词\n【词性槽】where 从句缺谓语动词，与 succeed 对照\n【依据】struggle 与 succeed 形成对照\nwhere ... children succeed in controlling their reputation and where they __(34)__：\nstruggle（挣扎、感到吃力）正是 succeed（成功）的反面，「在哪里成功、在哪里受挫」\n是典型的研究问题表述。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accepted", "B) audiences", "C) building", "D) complex", "E) constitutes", "F) deputies", "G) previously", "H) revolving", "I) samples", "J) selected", "K) solemn", "L) struggle", "M) suddenly", "N) understand", "O) vary"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) suddenly\n副词\n【词性槽】动词 pop 后修饰其方式的成分\n【依据】强调「凭空冒出」的反逻辑\nChildren don't just show up to the first day of kindergarten and have the idea of\nreputation pop __(35)__ into existence. 作者要否定的正是「名声观念会突然蹦出来」这\n种说法，suddenly 精准对应「突然冒出」。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "During the course of preparing for Design Ventura, teachers receive training and support while students get tutoring from designers.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】教师培训+学生由设计师辅导\n【定位】Along the way there is training and support for teachers and tutoring for students\nfrom designers.\n在这一过程中，老师能获得培训和支持，学生则由设计师提供辅导。\n【改写】teachers receive training and support ↔ training and support for teachers\n主动句↔存在句换位\nstudents get tutoring from designers ↔ tutoring for students from designers\n受动方与施动方都对得上", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "A visit to the Design Museum shop in London can reduce the designers' fears about the future supply of talents educated in Britain.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】参观博物馆商店缓解担忧\n【定位】However, if these designers had visited the Design Museum shop in London earlier this year\ntheir fears may have been eased.\n不过，如果这些设计师今年早些时候去过伦敦设计博物馆商店，他们的担忧本可以得到\n缓解。\n【改写】A visit to the Design Museum shop in London ↔\nif these designers had visited the Design Museum shop in London earlier this year\n名词化↔动词化\ncan reduce the designers' fears about the future supply of talents educated in Britain ↔\ntheir fears may have been eased\nreduce↔ease+talent复述前文\n【辨邻】 D D段讲的是这款玩具是2015年Design Ventura比赛获胜作品the toy was the winning\nentry in the museum's 2015 Design Ventura competition，并未提到'参观商店—\n缓解担忧'这条因果，仅与C段共享'设计博物馆'这个话题。\nthe toy was the winning entry in the museum's 2015 Design Ventura competition", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "One of Design Ventura's judges says the competition adds vigour to the standard curriculum in schools.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】评委说比赛给课程添活力\n【定位】Now that design education is limited in the curriculum, I'm all in favour of competitions such\nas Design Ventura, which helps to bring the standard curriculum to life.\n鉴于设计教育在课程中受到限制，我非常支持像Design Ventura这样的比赛，它能让标准课\n程焕发生气。\n【改写】One of Design Ventura's judges ↔ one of the competition's judges\nDesign Ventura=the competition\nadds vigour to the standard curriculum in schools ↔ bring the standard curriculum to life\n添活力=让其活起来\n【辨邻】 G G段说话人是博物馆学习部门主管Catherine Ritman Smith, head of learning at the\nDesign Museum，并非评委；她讲的是老师为何喜欢这个项目，话题相邻但身份与判\n断都对不上陈述句。\nTeachers like it because the skills are all transferable and it helps to validate the value of design as\na subject", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Enrollment in Design and Technology decreased by nearly ten percent last year in the UK.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】设计与技术报考人数降近10%\n【定位】These figures showed that the number of pupils that sat exams in Design and Technology fell\n10 percent from 204,788 to 185,279 last year.\n这些数据显示，去年参加设计与技术科目考试的学生人数下降了10%，从204,788人减至\n185,279人。\n【改写】Enrollment in Design and Technology ↔\nthe number of pupils that sat exams in Design and Technology\n报名人数=参加考试的学生数\ndecreased by nearly ten percent last year ↔ fell 10 percent from 204,788 to 185,279 last year\ndecrease↔fall+nearly↔精确数字", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Participation of ninth graders in Design Ventura can motivate them to choose design technology as their subject.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】九年级参赛促其选这门课\n【定位】We find that if pupils take part in Design Ventura in Year Nine it can be the trigger which\nleads them to opt for it at General Certificate of Secondary Education, so we are helping to\nkeep alive something which is crucial to industry and entrepreneurship.\n我们发现，如果学生在九年级参加Design Ventura，这可能成为一个契机，促使他们在英国\n普通中等教育证书阶段选修该科目，因此我们正在让一件对产业和创业都至关重要的事保持\n活力。\n【改写】Participation of ninth graders in Design Ventura ↔\nif pupils take part in Design Ventura in Year Nine\n名词化↔条件句\ncan motivate them to choose design technology as their subject ↔\nit can be the trigger which leads them to opt for it at General Certificate of Secondary\nEducation\nmotivate=trigger+leads to+opt=choose\n【辨邻】 E E段说话人Liam Hourican讲的是他作为老师为何引入比赛——I introduced it here\nbecause it helps to develop so many skills，停在'培养技能'层面，并没有讲学生因\n此选这门课的因果链。\nI introduced it here because it helps to develop so many skills", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Design Ventura is welcomed by teachers because it helps to prove the worth of design as a school subject.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】老师认可：证明学科价值\n【定位】Teachers like it because the skills are all transferable and it helps to validate the value of\ndesign as a subject.\n老师们喜欢这个项目，因为这些技能都可迁移，而且它有助于证明设计作为一门学科的\n价值。\n【改写】welcomed by teachers ↔ Teachers like it\n被动近义↔主动\nhelps to prove the worth of design as a school subject ↔\nhelps to validate the value of design as a subject\nprove↔validate+worth↔value\n【辨邻】 L L段是评委Naomi Cleaver的发言I'm all in favour of competitions such as Design\nVentura，讲的是评委对比赛的态度，并非'老师欢迎'，与陈述句锁定的态度主体对\n不上。\nI'm all in favour of competitions such as Design Ventura, which helps to bring the standard\ncurriculum to life", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The schools don't have to pay anything to take part in the Design Ventura competition.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学校参赛零费用\n【定位】It costs the schools nothing to participate in Design Ventura; the professional designers\nprovide their services for free.\n学校参加Design Ventura不花一分钱；专业设计师免费提供服务。\n【改写】The schools don't have to pay anything ↔ It costs the schools nothing\n付钱方视角↔成本方视角\nto take part in the Design Ventura competition ↔ to participate in Design Ventura\ntake part in=participate in", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Participants in Design Ventura are challenged to create sustainable and marketable products.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】可持续且可销售的产品\n【定位】Designed by a group of six Year 9 and Year 10 boys from Finchley Catholic High School, the\ntoy was the winning entry in the museum's 2015 Design Ventura competition, which brings\nthe business of design to life for students aged 13 to 16 by challenging them to develop a new\ncreative, sustainable and commercially feasible product for the Design Museum shop and\nattracts hundreds of entries nationwide.\n这款玩具由Finchley Catholic High School的六名9、10年级男生设计，是博物馆2015年\nDesign Ventura比赛的获胜作品；该赛事面向13到16岁的学生，挑战他们为设计博物馆商\n店开发一款全新、有创意、可持续且商业上可行的产品，吸引了全国数百份参赛作品。\n【改写】Participants in Design Ventura are challenged to create ↔ challenging them to develop\n被动主语化↔现在分词\nsustainable and marketable products ↔\na new creative, sustainable and commercially feasible product\n单词↔短语+sustainable原样复现\n【辨邻】 H H段讲的是赛事流程produce imaginative design ideas for a product in simple\nmaterials that could sell for £10，落脚点是'10英镑可售的设计构想'，没有\nsustainable+marketable的并列形容词描述，与陈述句对'产品属性'的聚焦不符。\nproduce imaginative design ideas for a product in simple materials that could sell for £10 in the\nDesign Museum shop", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Students benefit from Design Ventura because they can learn about budgeting and marketing in addition to game design.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】除游戏设计外还学预算和营销\n【定位】One of the really useful things about this competition is that there's a commercial element\nbecause the product is going on sale for real and the boys had to learn about budgeting and\nmarketing, as well as designing their game.\n这场比赛真正有用的一点是，它有商业元素：因为产品要真的上架销售，所以孩子们除了设\n计游戏，还得学预算和营销。\n【改写】budgeting and marketing in addition to game design ↔\nbudgeting and marketing, as well as designing their game\n并列项原样+连接词同义\nStudents benefit from Design Ventura because they can learn about ↔\nthe boys had to learn about\n受益与学到的事等值\n【辨邻】 H H段讲的是流程层面的支持there is training and support for teachers and tutoring\nfor students from designers，只到'设计师辅导'层面，没有触及\nbudgeting/marketing这类商业技能并列，与陈述句锁定的三连收益不符。\nAlong the way there is training and support for teachers and tutoring for students from designers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "According to an officer of the Creative Industries Federation, there is difficulty now in recruiting engineers in the UK.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】创意产业联合会官员说工程师难招\n【定位】He said, \"We already have skills shortages in many jobs such as animation(动画制作) and\nspecial effects.\" He added: \"Engineering, which requires a similar mix of creative and\ntechnical skills, also has recruitment problems.\"\n他说：'我们在动画、特效等许多岗位上已经面临技能短缺。'他还补充道：'工程领域需要相\n似的创意与技术混合能力，同样存在招聘困难。'\n【改写】an officer of the Creative Industries Federation ↔\nChief executive John Kampfner of the Creative Industries Federation\nofficer是chief executive的上位词\ndifficulty now in recruiting engineers in the UK ↔\nEngineering, which requires a similar mix of creative and technical skills, also has recruitment\nproblems\ndifficulty in recruiting engineers=Engineering has recruitment problems\n【辨邻】 A A段虽提到a leading design federation in the UK warned that the UK could face\na creative skills shortage，但落脚点是科目报考人数下降数据，并未由具体官员说'工\n程师难招'，与陈述句锁定的'官员发言+招工程师难'对不上。\na leading design federation in the UK warned that the UK could face a creative skills shortage", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from new research about pandas?", "options": ["A) They are losing habitat due to the building of roads and houses.", "B) They have stopped seeking new mates for reproduction.", "C) They may not adapt to the fragmentation of their habitat.", "D) They may cease to exist as a result of enjoying too good a life."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from new research about pandas? 问 \"new research\" 直接告诉了我\n们什么，是对研究结论原文的客观转述题。\n【拆句】题干 Q46 是一个标准 wh- 问句：What 作 learn 的宾语，from new research 是来源状\n语，about pandas 修饰 research，限定研究对象。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】New research suggests that pandas may be at risk of dying out because they are\ntoo comfortable.\n新的研究表明，熊猫可能因为生活得太舒适而面临灭绝的风险。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nlosing habitat due to the building of roads and houses 是 P2 中\nenvironmentalists \"long believed\" 的旧观点，并非新研究的结论。\nB) 过度推断\n原文是 pandas fail to wander off in search of new mates if they find their habitat\ntoo comfortable——条件状语限定，They have stopped seeking new mates for\nreproduction. 删掉条件、把可能性说成全体熊猫已经停止寻偶，与原文不符。\nC) 与原文相反\n新研究主张栖息地适度碎片化反而\"helps preserve\"熊猫，They may not adapt to the\nfragmentation of their habitat. 反过来说熊猫不能适应碎片化，与原文相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmay be at risk of dying out ↔ may cease to exist\nbecause they are too comfortable ↔ as a result of enjoying too good a life\nD 选项把\"太舒适 → 可能灭绝\"两层语义都对位地复述了一遍，与预判完全等值，可直接\n锁定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What can we conclude from the new research by scientists at Michigan State University?", "options": ["A) Environmentalists' long-time belief regarding panda conservation may be misleading.", "B) Housing development near pandas' homes may threaten their survival.", "C) Pandas' natural habitats are becoming less suitable for reproduction.", "D) The increased panda population is attributed to the fragmentation of their habitat."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】What can we conclude from the new research 中的 conclude 是推断/结论类信号词，\n要求在原文事实之上做一步合理推导。\n【拆句】What can we conclude from the new research by scientists at Michigan State\nUniversity? 中 by scientists at Michigan State University 是介词短语后置修饰\nresearch，限定研究来源；主干 What can we conclude 即 \"我们可以得出什么结论\"，结\n构简单。\n【定位】The research was conducted by scientists from Michigan State University. It concluded that\npandas fail to wander off in search of new mates if they find their habitat too comfortable,\nresulting in a lack of vital genetic diversity.\n这项研究由密歇根州立大学的科学家们开展。研究得出结论：如果熊猫觉得栖息地太舒服，\n它们就不会外出寻找新的配偶，从而导致缺乏关键的遗传多样性。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nEnvironmentalists have long believed that building roads or homes near the bears may threaten\ntheir survival\n↔ Environmentalists' long-time belief regarding panda conservation\nBut the new research suggests that a modest degree of discomfort and fragmentation may\nactually help preserve panda populations.\n↔ may be misleading\nA 项正是把 P2 的\"旧信念\"与 P3 新研究的反向结论合在一起得出的合理推断，与预判完\n全一致。\nB) 张冠李戴\nHousing development near pandas' homes may threaten their survival. 几乎是 P2\n旧观点的原文转述，不是新研究的结论。\nC) 无中生有\n原文只说舒适让熊猫不愿外出找新伴，未说栖息地\"不适合繁衍\"；Pandas' natural\nhabitats are becoming less suitable for reproduction. 是无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文是\"栖息地碎片化\"对熊猫\"种群延续\"有帮助，而非\"碎片化使熊猫数量增加\"。The\nincreased panda population is attributed to the fragmentation of their habitat. 把\n帮助保存说成数量增加，且因果说法不准确，与原文相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is the experts' conclusion regarding pandas?", "options": ["A) It is urgent to provide an ideal habitat for them to thrive.", "B) It is very important to preserve their genetic diversity.", "C) Their chances of finding new mates have a lot to do with their habitat.", "D) Their environment for survival has been continuously worsening."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the experts' conclusion regarding pandas? 问专家给出的明确结论，是对一段\n原文的客观复述。\n【拆句】What is the experts' conclusion regarding pandas? 中 regarding pandas 是介词短\n语作 conclusion 的修饰语，相当于 about pandas，限定结论的对象；句子是简单系表\n结构。\n【定位】The experts concluded that pandas should ideally \"be happy enough to thrive, but not so\ncontent that they don't want to move around and find new mates\".\n专家们得出结论：熊猫理想的状态应当是\"既能开心地繁衍生息，又不至于舒服到不愿四处\n走动去寻找新的配偶\"。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nP5 强调的是\"恰好的舒适度\"，不是\"提供一个理想的栖息地刻不容缓\"。It is urgent to\nprovide an ideal habitat for them to thrive. 中 urgent 是原文未提的紧迫态度，且\nideal habitat 把原文复杂的\"sweet spot\"过度简化。\nB) 张冠李戴\n遗传多样性出现在 P3 是机制说明，不是 P5 专家结论的内容。It is very important to\npreserve their genetic diversity. 直接把别处的细节当作\"专家结论\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nbe happy enough to thrive, but not so content that they don't want to move around and find new\nmates\n↔ Their chances of finding new mates have a lot to do with their habitat.\ntheir habitat（隐含于 happy/content 之中——\"舒适\"是栖息地状态） ↔ their habitat\nC 用\"栖息地与找新伴几率有关\"概括了预判中的两层逻辑，且未做超出原文的推论，是\n最贴的概括项。\nD) 无中生有\n原文只在 P2 提到旧观点中道路住宅可能威胁栖息地，但新研究并未说生存环境\"持续在\n恶化\"。Their environment for survival has been continuously worsening. 是无中\n生有。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What can we infer from the passage about the Goldilocks principle?", "options": ["A) It needs to be confirmed by more studies on pandas.", "B) It applies to the preservation of pandas too.", "C) It has implications for future panda research.", "D) It can be used to locate the right spot for pandas."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题）\n【判型】What can we infer from the passage 中 infer 是推断题的标志词，要求基于原文做一步合\n理推导。\n【拆句】What can we infer from the passage about the Goldilocks principle?：about the\nGoldilocks principle 是介词短语，作 infer 的宾语补足说明，相当于 \"关于 Goldilocks 原\n则\"。结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Their conclusions about what The Guardian described as this \"sweet spot\" are in line with the\nso-called Goldilocks principle: that there can be just the right amount of something.\n他们关于《卫报》所说的这个\"甜点位\"的结论，与所谓的 Goldilocks 原则一致：即任何事\n物都可以有一个恰到好处的量。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIt needs to be confirmed by more studies on pandas. 原文未提及；Goldilocks 原\n则是\"被沿用\"的概念，不存在\"还需进一步研究确认\"的语义。\nB) ✓ 正确\nTheir conclusions about what The Guardian described as this \"sweet spot\" are in line with the\nso-called Goldilocks principle\n↔ It applies to the preservation of pandas too.\napplied to a wide range of disciplines, from developmental psychology to economics and\nengineering\n↔ applies to … too\nB 用 applies to … too 精确表达了\"该原则同样适用于熊猫保护\"，是 in line with 一步\n推出的合理结论。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文是熊猫研究印证了原则，不是原则对熊猫研究有启示。It has implications for\nfuture panda research. 把作用方向反着写。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt can be used to locate the right spot for pandas. 把 sweet spot 误当成熊猫的地理\nsweet spot；原文的 sweet spot 是\"恰好的舒适程度\"的比喻，不是物理位置。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What can the new findings do according to Professor Sillero?", "options": ["A) Help discover new ways for the conservation of pandas.", "B) Help remove pandas from the list of endangered species.", "C) Shed light on the conservation of most large meat-eating animals.", "D) Show the complexity of interactions between humans and animals."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What can the new findings do according to Professor Sillero? 直接问 Sillero 教授的原\n话，是对引语原文的客观转述。\n【拆句】What can the new findings do according to Professor Sillero? 主干是 What can the\nnew findings do，according to Professor Sillero 是介词短语作来源状语；句中 do 是实\n义动词，被 What 提前作宾语。\n【定位】It may well be that the messy nature of their relationship with human efforts induces more\nanimals to scatter or travel further, and might result in greater genetic connectivity and\nenhanced population persistence.\n很可能正是它们与人类活动这种\"乱糟糟\"的关系，促使更多动物分散、走得更远，从而带来\n更强的遗传连通性，以及种群存续能力的提升。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nSillero 的话恰恰是说\"超出熊猫保护\"，Help discover new ways for the conservation\nof pandas. 把启示范围又拉回熊猫，与原文方向相反。\nB) 张冠李戴\nputting them on the list of vulnerable, but not endangered, species 只是说现状已\n不在 endangered 名单，并非新发现的功效。Help remove pandas from the list of\nendangered species. 把背景数据当作新发现的功效。\nC) ✓ 正确\nimplications beyond panda conservation ↔\nShed light on the conservation of most large meat-eating animals.\nMost large animals that eat meat live in increasingly fragmented landscapes ↔\nmost large meat-eating animals\nC 用 Shed light on + most large meat-eating animals 直接对应原文 implications\nbeyond panda conservation + Most large animals that eat meat，与预判完全\n一致。\nD) 偷换概念\nSillero 强调 induces more animals to scatter or travel further，给出的是积极效益，\n不是 Show the complexity of interactions between humans and animals. 那种中\n性\"展示复杂性\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the passage say about people born with natural talents?", "options": ["A) They seem to outdo others without hard work.", "B) They appear to know all the secrets to success.", "C) They feel it only too logical to succeed.", "D) They are bound to excel effortlessly."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about people born with natural talents 直接问文章对一个\n具体对象的陈述，没有 infer/imply/mainly about 这类字眼，是典型细节复述题。\n【拆句】主干是 What does the passage say about people，过去分词短语 born with natural\ntalents 作后置定语修饰 people，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】With those born with natural talents, it feels as if they excel without really trying.\n对于那些天生有才的人，看上去就好像他们不太费力就能出类拔萃。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nit feels as if they excel without really trying ↔\nThey seem to outdo others without hard work\n预判的「看似/不费力/胜过别人」三个语义点在 A 中一一落位，无增项无漏项，A 是定\n位句的同义改写。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只说他们看似不费力就出色，没说他们 know all the secrets to success——「掌\n握所有成功秘诀」是凭空添加的内容。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是「看上去毫不费力就成功」，选项却说 They feel it only too logical to succeed\n——「他们觉得自己成功理所当然」是从他们的主观视角描写，与原文「旁观者眼中显\n得轻松」不一致。\nD) 过度推断\n原文只用 it feels as if（似乎）表达印象，选项却用 are bound to（必然、注定）把可\n能性写成必然性，过度推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does Duckworth say about talent?", "options": ["A) It is a new concept much too overrated.", "B) It proves necessary for big achievements.", "C) It plays a lesser role in one's success.", "D) It is a guarantee for real success in life."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does Duckworth say about talent 问的是特定人物对特定对象的陈述，是细节题。\n【拆句】结构与 51 题平行：What does Duckworth say about talent，主谓宾完整，无修饰\n从句。\n【定位】As much as talent counts, effort counts twice.\n虽然才能很重要，但努力的重要性是它的两倍。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nIt is a new concept much too overrated 把 She introduces a new concept 中「新\n概念」的所指对象——其实是 grit——硬安到 talent 头上；原文说的「新概念」指\ngrit，而非 talent。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文反复说 talent 不够、需 grit 加持（Even if you have natural talent, it's nothing\nwithout grit）；选项却说 talent proves necessary for big achievements，是反向\n陈述。\nC) ✓ 正确\nAs much as talent counts, effort counts twice ↔ It plays a lesser role in one's success\ntalent may be overrated ↔ plays a lesser role\n预判的「才能不如努力重要、被高估」与 C 的「作用较小」在比较义上完全等值，C\n正确。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文称 grit 才是 real success 的关键（if you want real success, what you need is\ngrit），选项却说 talent is a guarantee for real success，把被弱化的对象说成「保\n证」，方向与程度同时反了。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the passage say about people thinking of attaining success?", "options": ["A) They are puzzled how to present their best to the employer.", "B) They are burdened by their expectation of perfection.", "C) They will try hard to land a job that fits their skills best.", "D) They will find themselves lacking in all the skills they need."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about people thinking of attaining success 仍是 say 句\n型，针对一个具体场景，是细节题。\n【拆句】主干仍是 What does the passage say about people，现在分词短语 thinking of\nattaining success 作后置定语修饰 people；其中 attaining 是动名词作 of 的宾语，整体\n「想着去获得成功的人」。\n【定位】When we think about attaining success—whether it's landing that job or learning that new\nskill—our thoughts are immediately burdened by all the things we must first learn. If you\nwant that new job, you have to learn the job skills, then the interview skills, then the dress\npart—and you must be perfect at all of them.\n当我们考虑获得成功时——不管是拿下那份工作还是学会某项新技能——我们的思绪立刻被\n「必须先学会的所有东西」压得沉甸甸的。如果你想要那份新工作，你得学工作技能，然后\n学面试技巧，再学穿衣得体——而且你必须在每一项上都做到完美。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nThey are puzzled how to present their best to the employer 把原文里「找工作」举\n例中的「dress part/interview skills」放大成全部困扰，且把 burdened 改成 puzzled\n——既缩小到一例又改写状态词。\nB) ✓ 正确\nour thoughts are immediately burdened by all the things we must first learn ↔\nThey are burdened by their expectation of perfection\n预判要求「负担 + 完美」两点齐备，B 正好同时命中——burdened 锁住「负担」，\nexpectation of perfection 锁住「完美主义」。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文是「想成功的人感到被各种要求压垮」，选项却说他们 will try hard to land a job\nthat fits their skills best——把焦虑负担改写成积极行动，方向反了。\nD) 过度推断\n原文说他们意识到要学很多东西，选项却说他们 lacking in all the skills they need\n——把「要学的东西多」推到「所有技能都欠缺」，all 是绝对化程度词，原文未明说\n欠缺。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "How does the author think grit can be helpful to us?", "options": ["A) It allows us to know what we are good at.", "B) It opens our eyes to new opportunities.", "C) It focuses our attention on what we do.", "D) It lets us reconsider the goals to achieve."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How does the author think grit can be helpful to us 问的是 grit 的具体作用，不是隐含\n推断，是细节题。\n【拆句】How does the author think grit can be helpful to us 是宾语从句嵌套结构：think 后接\n省略 that 的从句 grit can be helpful to us，how 修饰整个 think 的方式逻辑——实际问\n的是「作者认为 grit 以何种方式有帮助」。\n【定位】Grit lifts the unreasonable expectations off our shoulders. Grit tells us that the door is open\nwider than we first thought possible. Grit allows us to redefine our goals.\ngrit 把不合理的期待从我们肩上卸下来。grit 告诉我们这扇门比我们最初以为的开得更宽。\ngrit 允许我们重新定义自己的目标。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未说 grit 让我们 know what we are good at（知道自己擅长什么）；P4 反而提\n到「你一直想做但因『没技能』就放弃的事」，强调 grit 让你即便不擅长也去做，与\n「让你知道擅长什么」无关。\nB) 过度推断\n原文比喻 the door is open wider than we first thought possible，意思是给我们的\n可能性变多；选项 opens our eyes to new opportunities 把「门开宽」改成「让我们\n看见新机会」——「打开眼界」是额外添加的动作，原文只说门更宽、未说我们因此看\n见了新机会。\nC) 无中生有\n原文 Grit lifts the unreasonable expectations off our shoulders 是卸下「必须样样\n完美」的负担，而非聚焦注意力；选项 focuses our attention on what we do 与「卸\n担」无关，反而像「让你更专注」。\nD) ✓ 正确\nGrit allows us to redefine our goals ↔ It lets us reconsider the goals to achieve\nlet / reconsider / goals to achieve 三处都能在原文第三句找到一一对应，D 是 Grit\nallows us to redefine our goals 的同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2025-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What message does Duckworth try to convey in her book Grit?", "options": ["A) We should perfect ourselves to ensure success.", "B) We should stay persistent even in face of failures.", "C) We can never master a skill without constant practice.", "D) We can never expect to reach our goals without passion."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题）\n【判型】What message does Duckworth try to convey in her book Grit 问的是整本书的核心信\n息，是典型主旨题。\n【拆句】What message does Duckworth try to convey in her book Grit：宾语前置 What\nmessage，主干 Duckworth try to convey，介词短语 in her book Grit 作地点状语，无\n难点。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文反复说 perfection isn't the goal、Grit lifts the unreasonable expectations off\nour shoulders，明确否定完美主义；选项却让我们 perfect ourselves to ensure\nsuccess，方向反了。\nB) ✓ 正确\nIf at first you fail, then try one more time with grit ↔\nWe should stay persistent even in face of failures\ngrit, the perfect combination of passion and persistence ↔ stay persistent\ncontinue to try ↔ stay persistent\nB 同时命中预判的两大语义点——「失败」和「坚持」，是把全文主线压缩到一句话的\n最佳改写。\nC) 以偏概全\nEven if you haven't mastered a skill, grit tells you that you can still succeed 恰恰\n说「没掌握技能也能成」；选项却把全书主旨说成 never master a skill without\nconstant practice——把「关于 grit 的人生观」缩成「学技能要练习」，既偏题又用了\nnever 这种绝对化词。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文虽说 grit = passion + persistence，但全书主旨是「坚持着再试一次」，而非「没\n激情就达不到目标」；选项 can never expect to reach our goals without passion 用\nnever 把 passion 描述为达成目标的「唯一条件」，把成对要素中的一个单独绝对化。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
